    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_86fe4c75993534f72d6faac68eefa264ee3c7034c8677e3c30103243f2656b7c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Centering the partial Farey first moment by the observed local count h(ξ)/(2Φ(x)) separates where the points lie from how many have appeared by ξ. The resulting identity exposes both a location term and an explicit quadratic count term, alongside Bernoulli-weighted Mertens sums and S(x). This adaptive representation gains a visible count-versus-location decomposition, but it does not itself estimate any component, and its data-dependent center is not interchangeable with a fixed comparator without further analysis.",
